CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 4 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --array-rf
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
The over-constraining direction, and the one that caught a real bug.

a[idx] genuinely reaches 2, so the assertion is false and the answer must be
FAILED. An encoding that elects the wrong write proves it away instead and
reports SUCCESSFUL -- a missed bug, not a false alarm.

That is exactly what the first version of --array-rf did. The nested selection
was folded with the writes walked backwards and the rounds forwards, which put
the FIRST write at the LAST round outermost -- the zero initialiser -- so every
read was forced to 0. The candidates are now sorted by schedule position and
folded in increasing order, since the outermost ite is the one that wins.

An A/B over all 76 hard no-data-race tasks did NOT catch this: under --datarace
the verdict depends on the access events, not on what the accesses read, so the
whole family was blind to it by construction. This test is three lines and
caught it immediately.
